1215	1226	1196	1196	He	72	" What ? Lost again -- smothered o ' wit . "
1253	1262	1263	1263	he	3	" Now , look here , my lad , "
1266	1305	1263	1263	he	3	" some boys are born stupid , and thou art one of them ; some achieve stupidity -- that 's thee again , Jim -- thou wast both born stupid and hast greatly increased thy birthright -- and some "
1327	1370	1263	1263	he	3	" have stupidity thrust upon them , which , if it please the Lord , shall not be thy case , my lad , for I will thrust stupidity from thee , though I have to box thine ears in doing so , "
1420	1437	1410	1410	him	77	" Come hither , thou three - days - and - three - nights , thou , "
1480	1487	1488	1488	he	19	" I tell you , Edward , "
1494	1518	1488	1488	he	19	" old Pontifex was not only an able man , but he was one of the very ablest men that ever I knew . "
1533	1538	1539	1539	I	0	" My dear father , "
1542	1629	1539	1539	I	0	" what did he do ? He could draw a little , but could he to save his life have got a picture into the Royal Academy exhibition ? He built two organs and could play the Minuet in _ Samson _ on one and the March in _ Scipio _ on the other ; he was a good carpenter and a bit of a wag ; he was a good old fellow enough , but why make him out so much abler than he was ? "
1630	1634	1636	1637	my father	19	" My boy , "
1639	1739	1741	1741	he	19	" you must not judge by the work , but by the work in connection with the surroundings . Could Giotto or Filippo Lippi , think you , have got a picture into the Exhibition ? Would a single one of those frescoes we went to see when we were at Padua have the remotest chance of being hung , if it were sent in for exhibition now ? Why , the Academy people would be so outraged that they would not even write to poor Giotto to tell him to come and take his fresco away . Phew ! "
1746	1821	1741	1741	he	19	" if old Pontifex had had Cromwell 's chances he would have done all that Cromwell did , and have done it better ; if he had had Giotto 's chances he would have done all that Giotto did , and done it no worse ; as it was , he was a village carpenter , and I will undertake to say he never scamped a job in the whole course of his life . "
1822	1825	1827	1827	I	0	" But , "
1829	1868	1827	1827	I	0	" we can not judge people with so many ' ifs . ' If old Pontifex had lived in Giotto 's time he might have been another Giotto , but he did not live in Giotto 's time . "
1869	1876	1878	1879	my father	19	" I tell you , Edward , "
1884	2077	1878	1879	my father	19	" we must judge men not so much by what they do , as by what they make us feel that they have it in them to do . If a man has done enough either in painting , music or the affairs of life , to make me feel that I might trust him in an emergency he has done enough . It is not by what a man has actually put upon his canvas , nor yet by the acts which he has set down , so to speak , upon the canvas of his life that I will judge him , but by what he makes me feel that he felt and aimed at . If he has made me feel that he felt those things to be loveable which I hold loveable myself I ask no more ; his grammar may have been imperfect , but still I have understood him ; he and I are _ en rapport _ ; and I say again , Edward , that old Pontifex was not only an able man , but one of the very ablest men I ever knew . "
